WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.625
Welcome. In this class, you will turn
vague requests into

2
00:00:05.625 --> 00:00:11.250
prompts that another person can inspect,
reuse, and improve. We

3
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:16.875
will separate purpose, inputs,
instructions, constraints, deliverable,

4
00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:22.500
and verification. We will write observable
success criteria before asking

5
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.125
a model to generate. We will distinguish
requirements from preferences

6
00:00:28.125 --> 00:00:33.750
and examples. Finally, you will rewrite
one ambiguous request as

7
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:39.375
a reusable template. These techniques
apply across model providers because

8
00:00:39.375 --> 00:00:45.000
they clarify the work itself, not a
vendor-specific trick.

9
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.333
Begin with the useful outcome and the
person who will

10
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:55.667
use it. A topic is not yet a task. The

11
00:00:55.667 --> 00:01:01.000
request, customer feedback, leaves the
model and the reviewer to

12
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.333
guess whether the goal is a summary, a
sentiment count,

13
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:11.667
a product decision, or a response to each
customer. A

14
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:17.000
stronger purpose says, group the supplied
comments so the support

15
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:22.333
lead can choose three fixes. Now the
material, user, action,

16
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:27.667
and decision are visible. Purpose also
helps you decide whether

17
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:33.000
AI assistance is suitable. A private,
high-impact, or irreversible use

18
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.333
needs stricter data boundaries, evidence,
review, and stop conditions than

19
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:43.667
a reversible brainstorming draft. Before
adding detail, ask: who uses

20
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:49.000
the result, what will they do with it, and

21
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.333
what consequence follows if it is
incomplete or wrong? A

22
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:59.667
clear answer keeps later prompt choices
aligned with a real

23
00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:05.000
need instead of producing polished text
with no defined use.

24
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.333
Watch a purpose rewrite. The original
request is, make a

25
00:02:09.333 --> 00:02:13.667
plan for training. I will not begin by
adding decorative

26
00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:18.000
adjectives. I ask who needs the plan and
which decision

27
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.333
it supports. Suppose the user is a team
lead deciding

28
00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:26.667
how to onboard six analysts next month. I
ask what

29
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:31.000
evidence is available: role requirements,
current skills, schedule, and approved

30
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.333
learning resources. I ask what a usable
result looks like:

31
00:02:35.333 --> 00:02:39.667
a four-week table with outcomes,
activities, owners, and evidence of

32
00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:44.000
completion. I also name the consequence of
uncertainty: missing availability

33
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.333
must be flagged rather than invented. The
revised purpose becomes,

34
00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:52.667
use the supplied role requirements, skills
summary, schedule, and approved

35
00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:57.000
resources to draft a four-week onboarding
plan that helps the

36
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.333
team lead assign work and verify
readiness. This rewrite has

37
00:03:01.333 --> 00:03:05.667
not solved the task, but it has removed
several hidden

38
00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:10.000
decisions and made the next questions
obvious.

39
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Next, name the trusted inputs and separate
them from instructions.

40
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Inputs are the material the response may
analyze: a policy,

41
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.000
dataset, meeting notes, code, or examples.
Instructions say what to

42
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.000
do with that material. Use clear
boundaries so quoted documents,

43
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.000
web pages, and user-supplied text are
treated as data rather

44
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.000
than commands that can change the task.
State which sources

45
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.000
are authoritative and what to do when they
conflict. Use

46
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.000
ordered steps when sequence matters. For
example: first extract requirements

47
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
from the approved policy; second map each
proposed action to

48
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.000
one requirement; third list gaps without
filling them from memory.

49
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.000
Constraints name must, must not, privacy,
scope, and tool limits.

50
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.000
The deliverable names format and useful
detail. Verification names the

51
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.000
checks that occur after generation. If
required information is missing,

52
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.000
choose an explicit behavior: ask a
question, flag an assumption,

53
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.000
return an evidence gap, or stop. Those
labels make the

54
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:30.000
prompt easier to review and reduce silent
improvisation.

55
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.167
Take a prompt you have used or invent a

56
00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:38.333
simple one. Mark its purpose, input,
instruction, constraint, deliverable,

57
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:42.500
verification, and missing-information
behavior. If an element is absent, write

58
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:46.667
missing beside it. Do not improve the
prompt yet. The

59
00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:50.833
goal is to see which decisions the current
wording leaves

60
00:04:50.833 --> 00:04:55.000
hidden. Pause while you mark it.

61
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.706
Define success before generation so
quality does not depend on

62
00:06:29.706 --> 00:06:34.412
confidence or taste alone. An observable
criterion is something a

63
00:06:34.412 --> 00:06:39.118
reviewer can inspect: every required field
exists, each factual claim

64
00:06:39.118 --> 00:06:43.824
maps to supplied evidence, calculations
reproduce, links resolve, prohibited data

65
00:06:43.824 --> 00:06:48.529
is absent, or a stated rubric threshold is
met. Make

66
00:06:48.529 --> 00:06:53.235
it excellent is not observable. Concise is
incomplete unless you

67
00:06:53.235 --> 00:06:57.941
define a limit that still preserves
required content. Separate hard

68
00:06:57.941 --> 00:07:02.647
requirements from preferences. A hard
requirement can fail the result,

69
00:07:02.647 --> 00:07:07.353
such as using only the supplied policy or
returning all

70
00:07:07.353 --> 00:07:12.059
five required fields. A preference guides
style when it does

71
00:07:12.059 --> 00:07:16.765
not conflict, such as using short
sentences or a friendly

72
00:07:16.765 --> 00:07:21.471
tone. Examples demonstrate desired
behavior, but the governing rule should

73
00:07:21.471 --> 00:07:26.176
remain explicit; otherwise one easy
example may teach an accidental

74
00:07:26.176 --> 00:07:30.882
pattern. A useful review reads the result
twice: first against

75
00:07:30.882 --> 00:07:35.588
hard requirements and evidence, then
against preferences. This ordering

76
00:07:35.588 --> 00:07:40.294
prevents an attractive style from hiding a
missing fact or

77
00:07:40.294 --> 00:07:45.000
unsupported decision.

78
00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:51.250
Checkpoint. A prompt says, write a
brilliant comparison of three

79
00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:57.500
tools. Replace brilliant with two
observable success criteria and identify

80
00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:03.750
one preference. Your criteria must let
another reviewer decide whether

81
00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:10.000
the result passed without guessing your
taste.

82
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.500
A strong answer uses criteria such as:
compare all three

83
00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:32.000
tools against the same four named
requirements, and cite a

84
00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.500
supplied source for every current
capability claim. A preference might

85
00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:39.000
be, use a compact table followed by a
short recommendation.

86
00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.500
The first two can fail independently of
style. If your

87
00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:46.000
criteria used words like best,
comprehensive, or professional without

88
00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.500
defining evidence, revise them into a
count, field, mapping, test,

89
00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:53.000
or rubric that another person can inspect.
If you made

90
00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:56.500
the table format mandatory, explain why
the user or downstream

91
00:09:56.500 --> 00:10:00.000
workflow requires it; otherwise it may be
a preference.

92
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.000
A reusable prompt defines how uncertainty
becomes visible. Required context

93
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:10.000
can be missing, sources can conflict, or
the task can

94
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.000
require a choice the user did not
authorize. Decide which

95
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:20.000
branch applies. Ask a clarifying question
when the answer changes

96
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:25.000
the task and the user can provide it.
State a

97
00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:30.000
bounded assumption when stakes are low,
the assumption is visible,

98
00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.000
and the reviewer can replace it. Return
not supported by

99
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:40.000
the supplied evidence when a factual claim
lacks support. Stop

100
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.000
when continuing would cross a privacy,
permission, safety, or consequence

101
00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:50.000
boundary. Prompt detail improves the
probability of useful work; it

102
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:55.000
does not guarantee truth. Verification
still compares the generated result

103
00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:00.000
with original evidence, recalculates
important values, tests executable output,

104
00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.000
and keeps consequential action behind
appropriate authorization. Design the

105
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:10.000
failure path with the same care as the
happy path.

106
00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:15.000
A polished answer that hides an
unsupported choice is less

107
00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:20.000
useful than a clear request for missing
information.

108
00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.333
Now I will finish the training-plan
rewrite. Purpose: help the

109
00:11:24.333 --> 00:11:28.667
team lead assign a four-week analyst
onboarding plan. Inputs: only

110
00:11:28.667 --> 00:11:33.000
the supplied role requirements, skills
summary, schedule, and approved resources.

111
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:37.333
Instructions: identify gaps, sequence
activities, assign an owner, and map

112
00:11:37.333 --> 00:11:41.667
each activity to a role requirement.
Constraints: do not infer

113
00:11:41.667 --> 00:11:46.000
personal performance or add unapproved
resources. Deliverable: a table with

114
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:50.333
week, outcome, activity, owner, evidence,
and dependency. Hard success criteria:

115
00:11:50.333 --> 00:11:54.667
all required role capabilities appear,
every activity maps to a

116
00:11:54.667 --> 00:11:59.000
supplied requirement, and schedule
conflicts are flagged. Preference: use

117
00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:03.333
plain language. Missing-information rule:
ask if the start date or

118
00:12:03.333 --> 00:12:07.667
required roles are absent; otherwise mark
unknown availability rather than

119
00:12:07.667 --> 00:12:12.000
inventing it. Verification: the team lead
checks mappings and schedule

120
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.333
against the originals. Notice that the
example is reusable because

121
00:12:16.333 --> 00:12:20.667
its decisions are labeled. A different
user can replace the

122
00:12:20.667 --> 00:12:25.000
bracketed inputs without rediscovering the
contract.

123
00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:30.000
Open the rewrite activity. Choose a vague
request such as

124
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.000
summarize this, make a plan, or compare
these tools. Record

125
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:40.000
the original wording. Identify the user,
decision, trusted inputs, hard

126
00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:45.000
constraints, and observable success
criteria. Rewrite the request with

127
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:50.000
purpose, inputs, instructions,
constraints, deliverable, verification,

128
00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:55.000
and missing-information behavior. Then ask
another person, or use the

129
00:12:55.000 --> 00:13:00.000
supplied reviewer checklist, to name one
ambiguity removed and one

130
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.000
assumption that remains. Save both
versions and the review as

131
00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:10.000
your evidence.

132
00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:14.167
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

133
00:16:14.167 --> 00:16:18.333
identify a clear purpose, protect the
boundary between source text

134
00:16:18.333 --> 00:16:22.500
and instructions, choose observable
success criteria, define behavior for

135
00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:26.667
missing context, and explain why prompt
quality does not replace

136
00:16:26.667 --> 00:16:30.833
verification. Review the transcript or
revise your activity before submitting.

137
00:16:30.833 --> 00:16:35.000
The assessment opens only when you select
Begin knowledge check.

138
00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:38.750
Remember this rule: expose the decisions
before asking for the

139
00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:42.500
answer. Name the purpose, evidence,
requirements, uncertainty behavior,

140
00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:46.250
deliverable, and verification so useful
work can be reviewed and

141
00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:50.000
improved.
